{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Windows\\\\System32\\\\WindowsPowerShell\\\\v1.0\\\\powershell.exe\" -Command 'pdftotext -layout text_10p.pdf -'","aggregated_output":"Garden notes, page 1\r\n\r\nKeeping a small vegetable garden productive through a whole season is mostly a matter of steady,\r\nunglamorous habits rather than special tricks. The most common reason beginners give up is not\r\nbad soil or bad luck but an early burst of enthusiasm followed by weeks of neglect. A bed that gets\r\nten minutes of attention every second day will almost always outperform one that gets a whole\r\nweekend once a month.\r\n\r\nStart with the soil. Before planting, loosen the top twenty centimetres with a fork and mix in a\r\ngenerous layer of compost. Avoid digging when the ground is wet, because compacted clay dries\r\ninto hard clods that roots cannot push through. If water still pools on the surface an hour after rain,\r\nthe bed needs either raising or more organic matter, and usually both.\r\n\r\nChoose crops that suit the space and the climate instead of the seed catalogue. Lettuce, radishes,\r\nspinach and peas tolerate cool weather and can be sown early. Tomatoes, peppers, beans and\r\nsquash need warm soil and should wait until the risk of frost has passed. Sowing a short row of fast\r\ncrops every two weeks gives a steady harvest instead of a glut followed by nothing.\r\n\r\nWater deeply and less often. A thorough soaking twice a week encourages roots to grow down,\r\nwhile a light daily sprinkle keeps them near the surface where they dry out quickly. Water in the\r\nmorning so that leaves dry before evening, which reduces fungal disease. A layer of straw or\r\nshredded leaves around the plants keeps moisture in and weeds down.\r\n\r\nCheck the plants for pests while you water. Turning over a few leaves takes a minute and catches\r\naphids, caterpillars and slug damage before they spread. Many problems can be handled by hand:\r\npick off caterpillars, knock aphids off with a jet of water, and lay boards on the path overnight to\r\ncollect slugs in the morning. Healthy, unstressed plants resist most attacks on their own.\r\n\r\nFinally, keep simple notes. Write down what you planted, when, and how it did. After one season\r\nthe notebook becomes more useful than any general guide, because it describes your own soil,\r\nyour own weather and your own mistakes. Most experienced gardeners say the same thing: the\r\nsecond year is always better than the first, mainly because they remembered what went wrong.\r\n\fGarden notes, page 2\r\n\r\nKeeping a small vegetable garden productive through a whole season is mostly a matter of steady,\r\nunglamorous habits rather than special tricks. The most common reason beginners give up is not\r\nbad soil or bad luck but an early burst of enthusiasm followed by weeks of neglect. A bed that gets\r\nten minutes of attention every second day will almost always outperform one that gets a whole\r\nweekend once a month.\r\n\r\nStart with the soil. Before planting, loosen the top twenty centimetres with a fork and mix in a\r\ngenerous layer of compost. Avoid digging when the ground is wet, because compacted clay dries\r\ninto hard clods that roots cannot push through. If water still pools on the surface an hour after rain,\r\nthe bed needs either raising or more organic matter, and usually both.\r\n\r\nChoose crops that suit the space and the climate instead of the seed catalogue. Lettuce, radishes,\r\nspinach and peas tolerate cool weather and can be sown early. Tomatoes, peppers, beans and\r\nsquash need warm soil and should wait until the risk of frost has passed. Sowing a short row of fast\r\ncrops every two weeks gives a steady harvest instead of a glut followed by nothing.\r\n\r\nWater deeply and less often. A thorough soaking twice a week encourages roots to grow down,\r\nwhile a light daily sprinkle keeps them near the surface where they